WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.001 --> 00:00:07.080
"Efficacy and Safety of Fillers, Chemical Peels,
and Laser Skin Resurfacing"

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:10.380
The third most common
cosmetic procedures in the US,

00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:12.600
each done nearly
a million times a year,

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:16.200
are chemical peels
and laser skin "resurfacings,"

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:19.440
to create a "controlled
injury to the face."

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:22.680
The reasoning is that
the regeneration, repair,

00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:24.480
and remodeling of the damage

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:26.640
can result in a more
tightened appearance,

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:29.520
but may or may not
actually help with wrinkles.

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:34.440
The inflammation caused by these
types of facials causes edema,

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:37.800
fluid retention, in the face
that, because of the swelling,

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:39.600
can cause
a transient improvement

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:40.980
in the appearance
of fine wrinkles,

00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:44.280
but in the end may do
more harm than good.

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:49.080
Short-term side-effects include
bruising, swelling, itching,

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:52.980
crusting, redness,
infection, acne, and milia,

00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:54.540
which are little white cysts.

00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:58.500
Long-lasting side-effects can
include persistent redness,

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:00.960
pigmentation changes,
and scarring.

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:04.920
The second most common cosmetic
procedure, after Botox,

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:08.400
is volumizing injections
of soft tissue fillers,

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:11.520
carried out about 3 million
times a year in the US alone.

00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:15.900
Studies show subjective patient
satisfaction is often high,

00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:18.480
though long-term efficacy data
are lacking

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:21.060
because people tend to undergo
subsequent procedures.

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:24.720
Adverse outcomes occur
in about 1 in 40 procedures,

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:26.940
most commonly bruising,
discoloration,

00:01:26.940 --> 00:01:28.740
or unsightly lumps and bumps.

00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:31.860
Lumps are the most frequently
reported adverse event

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:33.180
reported to the FDA,

00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:34.440
followed by infection.

00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:37.800
It's important
to not apply makeup

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:40.140
or otherwise touch
the injection sites

00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:41.160
for at least four hours

00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:45.000
to prevent introducing bacteria
into the needle tracks.

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:48.420
The most devastating
filler complication

00:01:48.420 --> 00:01:50.640
is permanent blindness

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:53.820
due to an accidental
injection into an artery,

00:01:53.820 --> 00:01:55.800
which can cause skin necrosis

00:01:55.800 --> 00:01:57.840
but most seriously
can cause an embolus

00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:00.540
that lodges into
the ophthalmic artery

00:02:00.540 --> 00:02:02.640
and cuts off blood flow
to your retina.

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:04.680
This is exceedingly rare,

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:07.620
on the order of
1 in 1,000 nose injections,

00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:09.780
for example,
and in nearly all the cases.

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:12.180
the vision loss
was only in one eye.

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:15.840
There are some factors that
mediate the blindness risk.

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:18.180
Autologous fat injections--

00:02:18.180 --> 00:02:20.160
when they inject your own
liposuctioned fat--

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:21.600
seem to be the riskiest,

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:24.060
since they use a larger
volume and needle,

00:02:24.060 --> 00:02:25.740
though other facial injections,

00:02:25.740 --> 00:02:27.420
including platelet-rich plasma,

00:02:27.420 --> 00:02:30.360
have also resulted in
irreversible vision loss.

00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:33.780
To prevent this, medical
personnel are always taught

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:35.520
to aspirate a bit first,

00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:37.320
pull back the plunger
on the syringe

00:02:37.320 --> 00:02:39.840
to look for
that red flash of blood

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:41.280
that would indicate
you're in an artery.

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:43.740
Unfortunately this can give
a false sense of security,

00:02:43.740 --> 00:02:47.040
as tiny movements of the needle
can change its position.

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:51.300
The highest risk area is
the glabellar region,

00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:53.880
the skin between the eyebrows
and over the nose.

00:02:53.880 --> 00:02:57.360
Unfortunately there does not
appear to be a safe zone,

00:02:57.360 --> 00:02:59.400
since there's
a rich web of connections

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:00.900
among arteries
throughout the face.

00:03:00.900 --> 00:03:03.540
Though blindness
from filler injections

00:03:03.540 --> 00:03:06.480
has been said to occur
in any location on the face,

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:08.520
there do not appear
to be any cases

00:03:08.520 --> 00:03:11.040
arising solely
from lip augmentation.

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:14.160
Like with Botox, these days,

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:15.960
a large fraction
of filler injections

00:03:15.960 --> 00:03:17.940
are done by non-healthcare
professionals

00:03:17.940 --> 00:03:20.520
in a medical spa-type setting.

00:03:20.520 --> 00:03:23.040
This raises concerns
about illegal

00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:25.260
(non-FDA approved) fillers.

00:03:25.260 --> 00:03:27.360
There are reports of injections
with everything

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:30.240
from rubber cement
to Fix-a-Flat,

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:33.540
resulting in
disfigurement and even death.

